19:T4b6,## Add-ons without a class explosion

You sell coffee. Customers want espresso, espresso + milk, espresso + milk + sugar, … If you make a subclass per combination you get `EspressoWithMilkAndSugar`and the count explodes with every new add-on.

## Wrap instead of subclass

A **Decorator** is an object that **implements the same interface** as the thing it decorates AND **holds a reference** to one. It forwards the call to the wrapped object and adds its own bit:

```
Beverage drink = new Sugar(new Milk(new Espresso()));
drink.cost();   // 2.75
```

Each layer is transparent, to the client it's just a `Beverage`. Calls pass **inward** through the wrappers to the core, and results bubble **outward**, each decorator adding its part.

## Why it's powerful

- **Compose at runtime**, stack add-ons in any order, any number.
- **Open/Closed**, a new `Cream` decorator adds behaviour without touching existing classes.
- Single Responsibility, each decorator does one thing.

> The catch: many small wrapper objects, and `new Sugar(new Milk(new Espresso()))` can get noisy (a Builder or factory helps). Java I/O streams (`new BufferedReader(new FileReader(...))`) are the textbook real-world Decorator.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-structural","activeLesson":"decorator"}],["$","$L18","decorator",{"lesson":{"id":"decorator","patternId":"lld-structural","title":"Decorator","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Core","statement":"Add behaviour to an object by WRAPPING it in another object of the same type, stack as many layers as you like, at runtime.","companies":["Amazon","Starbucks","Google"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"A subclass per combination","code":["class EspressoWithMilk extends Espresso { ... }","class EspressoWithMilkAndSugar extends Espresso { ... }","// every new add-on doubles the subclasses 💥"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Modelling each add-on combo as a subclass means a class for every mix, milk, sugar, milk+sugar, …","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"esp","name":"Espresso","x":1,"y":0.1},{"id":"em","name":"EspWithMilk","x":0,"y":1.3,"tint":"red"},{"id":"ems","name":"EspMilkSugar","x":1,"y":1.3,"tint":"red"},{"id":"es","name":"EspWithSugar","x":2,"y":1.3,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[{"id":"b1","from":"em","to":"esp","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"b2","from":"ems","to":"esp","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"b3","from":"es","to":"esp","kind":"inherit"}],"shownClassIds":["esp","em","ems","es"],"shownRelIds":["b1","b2","b3"],"activeIds":["em","ems","es"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"Add caramel and the subclasses double again. This is the same combinatorial explosion inheritance always causes for behaviour that varies.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["esp","em","ems","es"],"shownRelIds":["b1","b2","b3"],"activeIds":["esp","em","ems","es"]},"vars":[["add-ons","exponential subclasses 💥"]]}]},{"label":"Wrap the beverage","code":["interface Beverage { double cost(); }","class Espresso implements Beverage { double cost(){ return 2.00; } }","abstract class AddOn implements Beverage {","    Beverage inner;                       // wraps another Beverage","    AddOn(Beverage b){ inner = b; }","}","class Milk  extends AddOn { double cost(){ return inner.cost() + 0.50; } }","class Sugar extends AddOn { double cost(){ return inner.cost() + 0.25; } }"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Beverage is the shared interface, just cost(). Espresso is the plain core component.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"bev","name":"Beverage","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ cost(): double"],"x":1,"y":0.1,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"addon","name":"AddOn","stereotype":"abstract","fields":["- inner: Beverage"],"methods":["+ cost()"],"x":1.5,"y":0.9,"tint":"accent2"},{"id":"esp","name":"Espresso","methods":["+ cost()"],"x":0,"y":1.85},{"id":"milk","name":"Milk","methods":["+ cost()"],"x":1.1,"y":1.85},{"id":"sugar","name":"Sugar","methods":["+ cost()"],"x":2,"y":1.85}],"relations":[{"id":"g1","from":"esp","to":"bev","kind":"implement"},{"id":"g2","from":"addon","to":"bev","kind":"implement"},{"id":"g3","from":"addon","to":"bev","kind":"aggregate","label":"wraps"},{"id":"g4","from":"milk","to":"addon","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"g5","from":"sugar","to":"addon","kind":"inherit"}],"shownClassIds":["bev","esp"],"shownRelIds":["g1"],"activeIds":["bev"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"AddOn is the key: it IMPLEMENTS Beverage (triangle) and also HOLDS a Beverage (diamond, “wraps”). A decorator both IS-A and HAS-A the thing it decorates.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["bev","esp","addon"],"shownRelIds":["g1","g2","g3"],"activeIds":["addon"]},"vars":[["decorator","is-a + wraps-a Beverage"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":6,"narration":"Milk and Sugar are concrete decorators, each adds its own cost on top of whatever it wraps.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["bev","esp","addon","milk","sugar"],"shownRelIds":["g1","g2","g3","g4","g5"],"activeIds":["milk","sugar"]}},{"view":"sequence","narration":"Now build: new Sugar(new Milk(new Espresso())). Calling cost() on the outer Sugar dives INWARD through the wrappers…","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"c","label":"Client","kind":"actor"},{"id":"sugar","label":"Sugar","tint":"accent2"},{"id":"milk","label":"Milk","tint":"accent2"},{"id":"esp","label":"Espresso"}],"messages":[{"id":"m1","from":"c","to":"sugar","label":"cost()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"sugar","to":"milk","label":"cost()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m3","from":"milk","to":"esp","label":"cost()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m4","from":"esp","to":"milk","label":"2.00","kind":"return"},{"id":"m5","from":"milk","to":"sugar","label":"2.50  (+0.50)","kind":"return"},{"id":"m6","from":"sugar","to":"c","label":"2.75  (+0.25)","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":1,"activeIndex":0},"vars":[["stack","Sugar → Milk → Espresso"]]},{"view":"sequence","narration":"Sugar asks Milk for its cost, Milk asks Espresso, the call passes down to the core.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:3:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:3:sequence:messages","shownCount":3,"activeIndex":2}},{"view":"sequence","narration":"Espresso returns 2.00; Milk adds 0.50 → 2.50; Sugar adds 0.25 → 2.75. Each layer contributes as the result bubbles OUTWARD. One call, stacked behaviour.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:3:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:3:sequence:messages","shownCount":6,"activeIndex":5},"vars":[["total","2.75 ✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
